UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 3: Consumer choices', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative you give up when you choose. It is one alternative, not every other thing in the shop. If you spend $15 on one item, the opportunity cost is the other $15 item you ranked second, not the entire catalogue.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nKim has $15 and chooses a jumper on sale for $15 instead of a $15 museum ticket. What is the opportunity cost, and what is it not?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up. It is not “everything else Kim might ever want”, and it is not $0 just because the jumper was on sale.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30. You only spend the $15 once. The cost of the choice is what you did not get.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nYou have $15. Choose between a $15 second-hand calculator you need for maths and a $15 cap. State your choice and name the opportunity cost in one sentence. Show that both options cost $15.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Commerce","learning_intention":"Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative you give up when you choose. It is one alternative, not every other thing in the shop. If you spend $15 on one item, the opportunity cost is the other $15 item you ranked second, not the entire catalogue.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CO5-DEC-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: You have $15. Choose between a $15 second-hand calculator you need for maths and a $15 cap. State your choice and name the opportunity cost in one sentence. Show that both options cost $15. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using scarcity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30.. Correction: The opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30. You only spend the $15 once. The cost of the choice is what you did not get. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating scarcity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with scarcity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about scarcity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for scarcity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for scarcity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story","Copy the model places and call that independent work"],"question":"Kim has $15 and chooses a jumper on sale for $15 instead of a $15 museum ticket. What is the opportunity cost, and what is it not?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for scarcity; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up","Careful use of opportunity cost with clear evidence","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30","Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named opportunity cost, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30","Name opportunity cost only, without linking it to scarcity","Apply scarcity to new material and name the opportunity cost used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies scarcity with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on scarcity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30","The opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up"],"question":"Which response best evaluates sources, places or historical/geographic evidence when analysing scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about scarcity","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Sam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name opportunity cost only, with no linked example","You have $15. Choose between a $15 second-hand calculator you need for maths and a $15 cap. State your choice and name","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30"],"question":"Which independent response best applies scarcity to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about scarcity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating scarcity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using scarcity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for scarcity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with scarcity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30.. Correction: The opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-commerce-y9-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 Commerce alignment to Commerce 7–10 (2024) outcome CO5-DEC-01. The syllabus says Stage 4 may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (CO4-ADJ-01). Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Not financial advice.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise scarcity.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Commerce","success_criteria":["I can use scarcity with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify opportunity cost and explain why it matters.","I can practise alternative independently on a new prompt.","I can notice trade-off needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited","A claim unrelated to the source or place"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the bottle. The opportunity cost is the three snacks, because that was the next best use of the same $15. Check: $5 × 3 = $15, so both options use the full amount.\n\nTRY FIRST\nKim has $15 and chooses a jumper on sale for $15 instead of a $15 museum ticket. What is the opportunity cost, and what is it not?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up. It is not “everything else Kim might ever want”, and it is not $0 just because the jumper was on sale.","explain":"Scarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative you give up when you choose. It is one alternative, not every other thing in the shop. If you spend $15 on one item, the opportunity cost is the other $15 item you ranked second, not the entire catalogue.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30. You only spend the $15 once. The cost of the choice is what you did not get. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Choosing the $15 bottle gives up three $5 snacks.","caption":"Opportunity cost is the next best alternative.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Choose</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">$15 bottle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Give up</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">three $5 snacks</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Sam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the b Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Sam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the b Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the bottle. The opportunity cost is the three snacks, because that was the next best use of the same $15. Check: $5 × 3 = $15, so both options use the full amount. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Both options cost $15; you do not add the prices together.","caption":"You spend the $15 once.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">$5 × 3 = $15</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">$15 + $15</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30. You only spend the $15 once. The cost of the choice is what you did not get. Here is a more productive model: Sam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the bottle. The opportunity cost is the three snacks, because that was the next best use of the same $15. Check: $5 × 3 = $15, so both options use the full amount.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe opportunity cost is the museum visit, the next best alternative given up. It is not “everything else Kim might ever want”, and it is not $0 just because the jumper was on sale.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking opportunity cost is the price tag plus the other item’s price, so $15 + $15 = $30. You only spend the $15 once. The cost of the choice is what you did not get. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about scarcity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nScarcity means you cannot buy every option, because money, time or stock is limited. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative you give up when you choose. It is one alternative, not every other thing in the shop. If you spend $15 on one item, the opportunity cost is the other $15 item you ranked second, not the entire catalogue. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 3: Consumer choices","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Consumer choices","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSam has exactly $15. Option A is a durable water bottle for $15. Option B is three $5 takeaway snacks. Sam chooses the bottle. The opportunity cost is the three snacks, because that was the next best use of the same $15. Check: $5 × 3 = $15, so both options use the full amount.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Choosing the $15 bottle gives up three $5 snacks.","caption":"Opportunity cost is the next best alternative.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Choose</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">$15 bottle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Give up</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">three $5 snacks</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Both options cost $15; you do not add the prices together.","caption":"You spend the $15 once.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">$5 × 3 = $15</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">$15 + $15</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-commerce-y9-w02-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
